    "available_at": "2026-11-09T20:29:54.809063+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Triumph TF-E Bikes Get Even More Enduro With Updates for the 2027 Model Year\nNovember Mini GT Preorder Reveals Six Fast Cars: One of Them Is a New Casting!\n20 photos\nPhoto: MINI GT\nI’ve been trying to dive deeper into the stories of various diecast manufacturers for over a year now. After Mattel’s Hot Wheels line I explored what Tarmac Works is all about, and I don’t want to stop there. I’ve got Greenlight and Mini GT on my agenda, but finding the right window for an interview seems to be quite complicated.\nI guess it will happen sooner or rather if I keep pushing for a meeting. Unboxing the company’s products can only take you so far, and online research almost never answers all the questions. Maybe I should try to get a hold of Jun Imai next, especially since I’ve started spending more time looking a Kaido House collectibles in recent months.\nAnyway, I was somewhat surprised to see a new pre-order post from Mini GT today. I thought to myself: “Didn’t we just have one recently?” Then I started connecting the dots and realized that we get a new selection of cars every two weeks. It’s typically a line-up of six collectibles, but we have had seven of them on the menu twice recently.\nThis predictable launch pattern works great if you ask me. You just need to find the patience required for pre-order items to be delivered from the factory. I only signed up for the first one a few months ago I believe, which means I should be able to enjoy the cars shortly. Alas, let’s focus on this first release for November.\nThere’s only one brand-new casting inside, and I’m thrilled to see it given I just recently reviewed NASCAR 26 on my PS5 . You might recognize the #24 Next Gen Garage 56 Chevrolet Camaro ZL1 that raced at the 2023 24hrs of Le Mans. This was a modified version of a Cup Series car, that Jenson Button, Jimmie Johnson and Mike Rockenfeller drove for 285 laps at the iconic event.\nPhoto: MINI GT They ultimately finished P39 out of 62 cars on the grid. It’s worth mentioning, however, that only 40 cars actually finished the race. Given my chronic nostalgia, I’d probably order one of these if it was a replica of a Gen 4 car instead as I’m still mentally stuck on motorsports from that day and age.\nAnyway, I’m sure this new casting opens up a long list of opportunities for future variations, considering the Next Gen cars have been around since 2022. This is the only American car on the list for today, as the other five machines will take us over to the old continent.\nWhile I acknowledge that the BMW M4 GT3 EVO is quite cool, it’s not nearly as exciting as the Lamborghini Huracan GT3 EVO2. I’m not familiar with either the two teams running these in their program, and that’s great.\nWhat Mini GT is doing (and I’ve seen this with Tarmac Works as well) is highlighting projects that take us away from the “hype zone” into a zone where we can learn more about motorsports and the history of racing from the comfort of our living room. The other BMW in this pre-order is a bit more relaxed, although certainly not short on power.\nPhoto: MINI GT I can’t recall exactly when the BMW M5 debuted, but I’m almost certain it sported the now iconic Isle of Man Green Metallic finish. There’s also an Alpine White version of the sedan to look for, plus a few iterations of the M5 Touring model . It’s the only item on the list where you can choose between a LHD or RHD model, which is one feature I don’t remember seeing any other 1/64th-scale manufacturer offering.\nNow that we’re done with Germany, let’s move over to the United Kingdom for the Jaguar C-X75 and the Aston Martin Valkyrie. Right off the bat, I can think of five previous variations for the Jag and I’m almost certain the Spectre replica has been the most desired one so far. It’s a car that deserves bright colors, which is my take for most of the machines that have an identity built around speed.\nI’m aware different people will have different opinions on the topic, which is why I don’t believe that the new version of the Jag can become a peg-warmer. The Valkyrie was also available in the previous pre-order. I didn’t like the Escaping White finish, and I’m not a fan of Cobalt Blue either.\nHyper Red, Sunburst Yellow, Maximum Orange and Aston Martin Racing Green were available before, and the Chase variations are the only ones that might “hurt” your bank account. I’m not even talking Hot Wheels Super Treasure Hunt figures here, so don’t worry about it.\nAs always, the pre-order window is only open for a relatively short period. And signing up for one (or all) of these means you’ll have to wait until Q2 of 2027 to have them delivered to your place. Until then, the market has plenty of readily-available options to choose from.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMINI GT diecast Aston Martin Valkyrie BMW M4 BMW M5 jaguar NASCAR lamborghini Huracan\nAbout the author: Dragos Chitulescu\nThe things Dragos enjoys the most in life are, in no particular order: cars, motorcycles, diecast cars, and drifting. He's seen (and driven) many vehicles since he started his writing career back in 2009, but his garage currently houses a 1991 Mazda RX-7 FC3S Turbo II and a 1999 Suzuki SV650-S. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2027 Chevrolet Silverado HD and GMC Sierra HD With the L8S Gas V8 Don't Feature a Dipstick",
